ht to be able to do that are the  Republicans really the party of school  choice and does that matter or is one  party better than the other is that the  wrong way to think of that school choice  I don't know if it's the wrong way to  think about it certainly has changed  over time so we were the Republicans  were the original party of school choice  for sure and I think Milton Friedman not  a Republican right but I had a great  honor to know him in my early days when  I started shaping my thinking about this  and so I'm a victim of that a happy  victim what Milton Friedman wrote about  the far superior results in a  marketplace driven by what people want  has informed the Republican Party about  school choice nowadays it's hard to tell  sometimes yeah let's talk about your  2013 memoir simple choices a subtitle is  thoughts on choosing environments that  support who your child is meant to be  talk a little bit about your the  personal story that you kind of work  through in that and it really gets to  that question of the moral case for  school choice right so I wrote that  because I wanted to write it while my  mother was still around as a tribute to  her and her message to us constantly was  look you got sent here for a reason and  our family that has to do with faith and  your obligation is to develop the  talents of the kids that you have and to  develop your own talents and then to  contribute you know to the community  that you live in my mother married my  biological father who then decided that  he wanted to get into the San Francisco  sort of beatnik scene and this was a guy  with everything right everything you  would need to contribute and he really  made a choice in his life not to do that  I didn't end up meeting him till I was  in my 40s and I tell this story in the  book good I met him at a homeless  shelter in San Francisco and  my objection was look you've got this  philosophy that's really Swift I love it  you you pursued your own needs you're  ac